    "https://osu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Columbus-Campus/Director--Translation-and-Venture-Development_R157870-1": {
      "title": "Director, Translation and Venture Development",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nScreen reader users may encounter difficulty with this site. For assistance with applying, please contact hr-accessibleapplication@osu.edu . If you have questions while submitting an application, please review these frequently asked questions . Current Employees and Students: If you are currently employed or enrolled as a student at The Ohio State University, please l og in to Workday to use the internal application process. Welcome to The Ohio State University's career site.  We invite you to apply to positions of interest. In order to ensure your application is complete, you must complete the following: Ensure you have all necessary documents available when starting the application process. You can review the additional job description section on postings for documents that may be required. Prior to submitting your application, please review and update (if necessary) the information in your candidate profile as it will transfer to your application.  Job Title: Director, Translation and Venture Development Department: ERIK | Technology Commercialization Position Overview The Director of Translation and Venture Development provides strategic leadership for Ohio State's research translation and venture development activities as a co-equal pillar alongside Licensing and Operations and Innovation Investment Management. This role is responsible for advancing innovations from invention disclosure through translational development, venture formation, and capital readiness—ultimately transforming university research into commercially viable ventures that create societal impact. Working in integrated partnership with the Director of Licensing and Operations and the Director of Innovation Investment Management, the Director leads programs, services, and ecosystem partnerships that increase commercialization readiness and venture creation. Opportunities enter the innovation pipeline through Licensing, with Translation and Venture Development providing structured support for translational advancement, venture development, and commercialization readiness. Together, the three pillars operate through an integrated governance framework that aligns resource allocation, milestone advancement, and commercialization outcomes. This position has direct authority over venture formation strategy, founder development, and translation portfolio decisions. It operates in close coordination with Licensing & Operations on intellectual property strategy and with Innovation Investment Management on investment readiness, with explicit decision frameworks to ensure rapid progression without compromising due diligence. Direct leadership of functions including Translation Program Manager, Program Specialist, Venture Development Lead, Venture Analyst, Venture Platform & Ecosystem Engagement Lead, and Expert Talent Coordinator. Oversight may also include EIRs, MIRs, Technical Project Managers, Student Associates, and external subject matter experts. Systematically removes barriers to entrepreneurship—bureaucratic friction, decision delays, and structural obstacles. Empower innovators and team with rapid decision-making and clear pathways to venture formation. Operates as a co-equal member of the Innovation and Commercialization leadership team, reporting to the Senior Associate Vice President, Innovation and Strategic Ventures, and collaborates closely with the Sr. Director of Licensing and Operations and the Director of Innovation Investment Management to ensure effective coordination and integrated decision-making across the commercialization ecosystem. Measures of Success Shared Commercialization Outcomes Increased invention-to-translation-project conversion (with defined cycle-time targets: baseline vs. target). Increased progression of funded projects through commercialization milestones within planned timelines (% on schedule). Increased transition of translation projects into licensing opportunities and venture formation. Increased venture formation with strong founder teams and validated business models. Improved venture-stage commercialization cycle time and speed to market. Strong integrated collaboration across Translation and Venture Development, Licensing and Operations, and Innovation Investment Management, with clear decision frameworks and rapid resolution of conflicts Increased follow-on translational and private capital raised by portfolio ventures. Translation & Venture Development Outcomes Increased faculty participation in translation and venture development programs, with diversity goals for founder demographics. Expanded mentor, entrepreneur, investor, alumni, and industry engagement; growth in EIR/MIR participation and quality; Increased number of commercialization-ready opportunities progressing to venture formation. Successful completion of stage-gated project milestones, with [X]% of ventures achieving funding/revenue milestones within 18 months of launch. Increased utilization of venture development resources and founder education programs. Reduction in decision-making cycle time for translation assessments and venture-readiness determinations (target: 60 days or less). Growth in external innovation ecosystem partnerships, venture support capacity, and Ohio State's reputation as a venture-creation institution. Position Impact The Director of Translation and Venture Development plays a critical role in advancing Ohio State's research, innovation, and economic development mission by building systems that connect discovery, talent, capital, and partnerships. As a co-equal strategic pillar within the Enterprise for Research, Innovation and Knowledge's (ERIK) commercialization ecosystem, this position drives the advancement of university innovations through structured translation and founder development, transforming promising research discoveries into commercially viable ventures that create lasting societal impact. Through empowerment of faculty entrepreneurs, rapid decision-making, systematic risk reduction, and strategic capital deployment, this leader helps position Ohio State as a premier research university that drives economic innovation and catalyzes transformative change. Required Qualifications Bachelor's degree. Minimum 10 years of progressively responsible leadership experience in building or scaling translation programs, venture development, technology transfer, or dual-pillar commercialization functions. Demonstrated track record managing translational research funding programs (NIH R21/R33, NSF SBIR/STTR, or private translation funds) OR launching and scaling ventures in biotech, medtech, deep technology, or advanced manufacturing. Demonstrated success leading complex initiatives, developing partnerships, and managing innovation portfolios or programs with measurable commercialization outcomes. Ability to operate in a matrix environment, navigate competing stakeholder priorities, and make rapid decisions in ambiguous situations. Desired Qualifications Advanced degree preferred (MBA, PhD, MD, JD, or related discipline). 15 or more years of relevant experience. Experience within a major research university, academic medical center, venture-backed startup, accelerator, incubator, or deep-tech investment organization. Familiarity with venture capital, angel investing, SBIR/STTR programs, translational funding, and faculty entrepreneurship ecosystems. Demonstrated expertise in regulatory pathways relevant to at least one of Ohio State's portfolio domains (biomedical, medtech, pharma, engineering, hardware, advanced manufacturing, or life sciences). Direct experience building or managing founder education programs, executive-in-residence (EIR) models, or innovation ecosystem partnerships. Application Materials A resume is required to be submitted at the time of application to be considered for this position. A cover letter is strongly desired. Additional Information: Salary The salary range for this position is $163,000.00 - $213,450.00. The offer for this position may fall within this range based on multiple factors including internal equity, unit’s available budget, and the selected candidate’s qualifications. Work Location This is a fully in-person position. Additional Information Due to U.S. export control regulations governing information, technology, and facilities accessed during the performance of work, applicants must be: (1) U.S. citizens or nationals; (2) permanent residents; (3) asylees or refugees; or (4) individuals otherwise described as “protected individuals” pursuant to Title 8 U.S. Code Section 1324b(a)(3)(b). Ohio State will not sponsor applicants for work visas for this position. ERIK Overview Join the Enterprise for Research, Innovation and Knowledge (ERIK) at The Ohio State University and become a part of a dynamic, forward-thinking team dedicated to pushing the boundaries of discovery and innovation. ERIK positions Ohio State to expand curiosity-driven research and creative expression, further develop our research community of faculty, staff, and students, and grow the innovation ecosystem to address big challenges and improve communities locally and globally. We are seeking passionate individuals who are committed to making a meaningful impact through collaboration, innovation, and excellence. To learn more about our mission and work, please visit https://erik.osu.edu/ . Position Mapping Function: Business Planning and Operations Sub function: Venture Development Career Band and Level: M4 Location: Energy Advancement and Innovation Center (1044) Position Type: Regular Scheduled Hours: 40 Shift: First Shift Final candidates are subject to successful completion of a background check.  A drug screen or physical may be required during the post offer process. Thank you for your interest in positions at The Ohio State University and Wexner Medical Center. Once you have applied, the most updated information on the status of your application can be found by visiting the Candidate Home section of this site. Please view your submitted applications by logging in and reviewing your status. For answers to additional questions please review the frequently asked questions . The university is an equal opportunity employer, including veterans and disability."
    },
    "https://osu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Columbus-Campus/Research-Assistant_R158656-1": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nScreen reader users may encounter difficulty with this site. For assistance with applying, please contact hr-accessibleapplication@osu.edu . If you have questions while submitting an application, please review these frequently asked questions . Current Employees and Students: If you are currently employed or enrolled as a student at The Ohio State University, please l og in to Workday to use the internal application process. Welcome to The Ohio State University's career site.  We invite you to apply to positions of interest. In order to ensure your application is complete, you must complete the following: Ensure you have all necessary documents available when starting the application process. You can review the additional job description section on postings for documents that may be required. Prior to submitting your application, please review and update (if necessary) the information in your candidate profile as it will transfer to your application.  Job Title: Research Assistant Department: Dentistry | Pediatric Dentistry The Ohio State University College of Dentistry is seeking a motivated Research Assistant in pediatric dentistry with a documented interest in medical/dental integration and Medicaid policy to join Dr. Beau Meyer’s research team. This is an exciting opportunity to contribute to new research focused on improving pediatric oral health, with an emphasis on addressing oral health disparities through health systems interventions aimed at improving care for underserved populations. This position is a term position for 1 year; it may be renewable based on performance and availability of future funding. The successful candidate will work under Dr. Meyer’s direction to complete data collection for ongoing research projects, including, but not limited to: Evaluating medical/dental integration as a socio-behavioral health systems intervention Evaluating the impact of Medicaid policies on oral health outcomes Application Instructions: To be considered, please submit your application electronically via Workday. Application materials must include a cover letter outlining your research and career interests, and a detailed CV. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled. For additional information, please contact Dr. Beau Meyer at [meyer.781@osu.edu]. The final candidate must successfully complete a background check and drug and alcohol screening. Work Schedule: Part-time, flexible work schedule, minimum expectation of 15hrs/week, up to 25hrs/week. Pay Range : $19.28 - 25.19 (based on experience and education) Additional Information: Experience and Education: Required: A bachelor's degree in dentistry or dental hygiene or equivalent experience in a dental clinic setting. This is a non-clinical position, but dental clinical knowledge is essential to the duties required of the job. Desired: Proficiency in literature reviews. Familiarity with oral health programs for underserved communities. Excellent communication and teamwork abilities. Location: Postle Hall (0024) Position Type: Temporary (Fixed Term) Scheduled Hours: 25 Shift: First Shift Final candidates are subject to successful completion of a background check.  A drug screen or physical may be required during the post offer process. Thank you for your interest in positions at The Ohio State University and Wexner Medical Center. Once you have applied, the most updated information on the status of your application can be found by visiting the Candidate Home section of this site. Please view your submitted applications by logging in and reviewing your status. For answers to additional questions please review the frequently asked questions . The university is an equal opportunity employer, including veterans and disability."
    },
    "https://osu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Wooster-Campus/Post-Doctoral-Scholar_R157959-1": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Scholar",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nScreen reader users may encounter difficulty with this site. For assistance with applying, please contact hr-accessibleapplication@osu.edu . If you have questions while submitting an application, please review these frequently asked questions . Current Employees and Students: If you are currently employed or enrolled as a student at The Ohio State University, please l og in to Workday to use the internal application process. Welcome to The Ohio State University's career site.  We invite you to apply to positions of interest. In order to ensure your application is complete, you must complete the following: Ensure you have all necessary documents available when starting the application process. You can review the additional job description section on postings for documents that may be required. Prior to submitting your application, please review and update (if necessary) the information in your candidate profile as it will transfer to your application.  Job Title: Post Doctoral Scholar Department: FAES | Plant Pathology Conducts independent research focused on different aspects of soil and plant health, including measurements of soil physicochemical characteristics, microbial diversity and activity, other soil food web components, and plant disease assessments. Specifically, the candidate leads research including experimental design, planning, setup and sampling, data generation and analysis. Experiments could include handling and processing of samples for soil, plant and microbial analyses (including nematodes), maintenance and identification of cultures/isolated populations, and environmental nucleic acid analysis. The candidate also prepares manuscripts and presentations deriving from interdisciplinary projects on soil health in Ohio cropping systems. The candidate will maintain thorough records of research, including annotated electronic resources and application of statistical analysis and bioinformatic pipelines. The candidate will be expected to contribute to other group activities, such as, but not limited to, training of laboratory personnel, general lab activities and discussions, and extension/outreach. Essential Duties: 50%  -  Collects, organizes, analyzes, interprets and communicates data (written and oral), including bioinformatic and statistical analysis of soil and plant health and microbiome datasets. 40%  -  Designs and conducts field, greenhouse and laboratory experiments, including characterization of plant and soil health parameters and analysis of soil food web components 10%  -  Advises and interact with graduate and undergraduate students in research activities. Additional Information: Degree Requirements : PhD with a major in Plant Pathology, Microbiology, Soil Sciences, Ecology, Statistics, Agronomy/Horticulture, or related field. Required Experience : Handling  microbial cultures and molecular expertise (such as DNA/RNA extraction and PCR) and nucleic acid sequence data analysis, including phylogenetics Soil and plant health assessments Experimental design and statistics Bioinformatics as it applies to plant microbiome analysis and familiarity with R language Manuscript preparation and publication Location: Selby Hall (0410) Position Type: Term (Fixed Term) Scheduled Hours: 40 Shift: First Shift Final candidates are subject to successful completion of a background check.  A drug screen or physical may be required during the post offer process. Thank you for your interest in positions at The Ohio State University and Wexner Medical Center. Once you have applied, the most updated information on the status of your application can be found by visiting the Candidate Home section of this site. Please view your submitted applications by logging in and reviewing your status. For answers to additional questions please review the frequently asked questions . The university is an equal opportunity employer, including veterans and disability."
    },
    "https://osu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/College-of-MedicineOffice-of-Health-Sciences/Standardized-Patient-Program-Senior-Specialist--CSEAC_R158157-2": {
      "title": "Standardized Patient Program Senior Specialist- CSEAC",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nScreen reader users may encounter difficulty with this site. For assistance with applying, please contact hr-accessibleapplication@osu.edu . If you have questions while submitting an application, please review these frequently asked questions . Current Employees and Students: If you are currently employed or enrolled as a student at The Ohio State University, please l og in to Workday to use the internal application process. Welcome to The Ohio State University's career site.  We invite you to apply to positions of interest. In order to ensure your application is complete, you must complete the following: Ensure you have all necessary documents available when starting the application process. You can review the additional job description section on postings for documents that may be required. Prior to submitting your application, please review and update (if necessary) the information in your candidate profile as it will transfer to your application.  Job Title: Standardized Patient Program Senior Specialist- CSEAC Department: Medicine | Education Clinical Skills Education and Assessment Center The Clinical Skills Education and Assessment Center (CSEAC), within the College of Medicine, is a multi-disciplinary professional skills training lab that provides education and hands-on experience in basic clinical skills and advanced surgical, ultrasound and medical scenarios in a simulated patient care setting for students, residents, medical trainees, faculty and staff from the health system, all health sciences schools and colleges, and external clients.  Services include medical simulation and standardized patient programs. The Standardized Patient Program Senior Specialist provides expertise and comprehensive support for the Standardized Patient (SP) Program within CSEAC. This individual serves as a primary point of contact, providing direction and information to all faculty, learners, and staff and provides support for general or specialized areas for learners including undergraduate, graduate, post-graduate, professional and independently practicing providers ensuring support throughout the educational cycle from entry to graduation and into professional practice. The individual provides support in all aspects of the SP program including educational functions, coordination of all SP-related activities from recruitment, on-boarding, training, and coaching of SPs to coordinating with faculty and program leadership to plan, adapt, maintain, and implement SP case materials to meet curricular needs.  Provides recommendations to program leadership on SP program strategy, standards of practice, and continuous improvement initiatives. Additionally, the individual oversees student employees and ensuring compliance with organizational policies and accreditation standards of best practice. Education and Experience Bachelor's degree or equivalent experience. Advanced degree in relevant field may be desirable. 4 years of relevant experience required. 4-8 years of relevant experience preferred. Experience in a medical educational or clinical setting preferred. Additional Information: Work hours are typically 8:00 a.m. – 5:00 p.m. Monday - Friday, but can vary to include some early morning, evening and weekend events as needed. Location: Meiling Hall (0281) Position Type: Regular Scheduled Hours: 40 Shift: First Shift Final candidates are subject to successful completion of a background check.  A drug screen or physical may be required during the post offer process. Thank you for your interest in positions at The Ohio State University and Wexner Medical Center. Once you have applied, the most updated information on the status of your application can be found by visiting the Candidate Home section of this site. Please view your submitted applications by logging in and reviewing your status. For answers to additional questions please review the frequently asked questions . The university is an equal opportunity employer, including veterans and disability."
    },
    "https://osu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/College-of-MedicineOffice-of-Health-Sciences/Education-Program-Specialist--Education_R157274-1": {
      "title": "Education Program Specialist- Education",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nScreen reader users may encounter difficulty with this site. For assistance with applying, please contact hr-accessibleapplication@osu.edu . If you have questions while submitting an application, please review these frequently asked questions . Current Employees and Students: If you are currently employed or enrolled as a student at The Ohio State University, please l og in to Workday to use the internal application process. Welcome to The Ohio State University's career site.  We invite you to apply to positions of interest. In order to ensure your application is complete, you must complete the following: Ensure you have all necessary documents available when starting the application process. You can review the additional job description section on postings for documents that may be required. Prior to submitting your application, please review and update (if necessary) the information in your candidate profile as it will transfer to your application.  Job Title: Education Program Specialist- Education Department: Medicine | Education Vice Dean Job Description The Education Program Specialist in the Office of Curriculum and Scholarship (OCS) within the College of Medicine plans and coordinates faculty development activities; supports educational technology, instructional design, program evaluation, and educational research services offered by OCS, oversees, organizes and coordinates daily activities and functions to ensure office success; and provides administrative, logistical and marketing support. Minimum Education Required Bachelor's Level Degree in education, communication or related field or equivalent combination of education and experience Required Qualifications 2 years of relevant experience required. Program planning and administration required, preferably in a higher education setting preferred; experience in the planning and organization of workshops and conferences preferred; experience with MS Office preferred; experience developing program and instructional materials desired. Requires successful completion of a background check. Selected candidate may be requested to complete a pre-employment physical including a drug screen. Function:   Education Sub Function: Education Program Career Band: S Career Level: S2 Additional Information: Location: Prior Hall (0302) Position Type: Regular Scheduled Hours: 40 Shift: First Shift Final candidates are subject to successful completion of a background check.  A drug screen or physical may be required during the post offer process. Thank you for your interest in positions at The Ohio State University and Wexner Medical Center. Once you have applied, the most updated information on the status of your application can be found by visiting the Candidate Home section of this site. Please view your submitted applications by logging in and reviewing your status. For answers to additional questions please review the frequently asked questions . The university is an equal opportunity employer, including veterans and disability."
